<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sweet-potato">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sweet potato</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sweet potatoes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> is a starchy root crop native to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> that spread across the world after the sixteenth century through Spanish and Portuguese trade networks. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> it was one of several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">New World crops</a>, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peanut">peanuts</a>, that allowed cultivation of marginal hill and upland land. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> link these crops to the dramatic population growth of Qing China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sweet-potato.jpg" alt="Sweet potato" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Llez · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ipomoea_batatas_006.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> (Ipomoea batatas) originated in the warm tropical regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, in what is now Ecuador, and was domesticated in either Central or South America. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> domesticated sweet potatoes were present at least 5,000 years ago, and local peoples most likely spread them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and South America by 2500 BCE. The oldest radiocarbon-dated remains known, from caves in the Chilca Canyon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, are about 8080 BCE old. Ceramics modelled after sweet potatoes are often found in the Moche culture.</p><p>Before Europeans reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, the sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> was grown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> and has been radiocarbon-dated in the Cook Islands to 1210-1400 CE. A common hypothesis is that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> who had travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and back brought a cutting to central Polynesia. Genetic similarities with indigenous Americans such as the Zenú, and the likeness of the Polynesian word kumala, as in Māori kūmara, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> k'umara, point the same way, and the present scholarly consensus favours pre-Columbian contact.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The plant is a herbaceous perennial vine whose stems crawl on the ground and root at the nodes. Its large, starchy, sweet-tasting tuberous roots are eaten as a root vegetable, and the young shoots and leaves as greens. It belongs to the morning glory family and is only distantly related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a>, and the darker sweet potatoes called yams in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> are even more distant from true yams. The plant does not tolerate frost and grows best at an average temperature of 24 °C. It is propagated by cuttings or by shoots called slips, and the roots mature in two to nine months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The first Europeans to taste sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> were members of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>'s expedition in 1492. The name that stayed was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> batata, which the Spanish combined with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> word for potato, papa, to make patata for the common potato. In some tropical areas the roots are a staple food, usually cooked, though the American colonists of the Southeast ate them raw. In northeastern Uganda sun-dried slices, amukeke, are a staple, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> the leaves and vine tips are widely eaten as a vegetable. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> the juice of red sweet potatoes mixed with lime juice dyes cloth in shades from pink to black.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sweet potato” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rwanda's economy rested on intensive hoe farming of sorghum, beans and bananas, later joined by American crops such as maize and <b>sweet potatoes</b>, combined with the herding of long-horned cattle that carried great social prestige.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-rwanda">Kingdom of Rwanda · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy rested on agriculture: rice, wheat and millet, supplemented by cotton, sericulture and, from the late 16th century, New World crops such as maize and <b>sweet potatoes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>This growth was aided by long internal peace and by New World crops, particularly maize, <b>sweet potatoes</b> and peanuts, which extended cultivation into previously marginal hill and upland territories.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The island had no permanent streams, and farming depended on careful technique: islanders spread broken stone over their fields to keep in moisture and improve fertility, and grew <b>sweet potatoes</b> above all, with bananas, sugar cane and taro, while chickens, fish and seabirds supplied protein.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rapa-nui">Rapa Nui · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>More than two thirds of households farm: matoke bananas cover over a third of the farmland, with potatoes, beans, <b>sweet potatoes</b>, cassava and maize; coffee and tea are the cash crops, more than 400,000 people living from coffee.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rwanda">Rwanda · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sweet potato</b>, maize, squash, beans, peppers, peanuts and pineapples were grown too, and the Taíno fished with nets, weirs and spears, hunted hutias, birds and iguanas, and kept fish and turtles alive in weirs until they were eaten.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Society</a></li></ul></section>
